1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:07,770
A prescription says amoxicillin clavulanate, 400 milligrams per 5 millilitres, 5 millilitres twice a day.

2
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:14,237
The bottle says 250: same drug, wrong concentration, and the child's dose falls below the WHO range.

3
00:00:14,267 --> 00:00:17,759
WHO puts medication errors at 42 billion dollars a year.

4
00:00:17,789 --> 00:00:23,504
RxLint checks the medicine being handed over against the prescription, the way a compiler checks code.

5
00:00:24,158 --> 00:00:27,708
Two photos, or a FHIR prescription and a bottle photo.

6
00:00:27,738 --> 00:00:38,328
On Nebius Token Factory, a vision model transcribes each photo, and NVIDIA Nemotron 3 Nano turns the transcript into typed facts, each citing the line it was copied from.

7
00:00:38,358 --> 00:00:43,850
PP-OCRv6 checks high-risk numbers, with a calibrated second gate.

8
00:00:43,880 --> 00:00:48,002
Then a deterministic kernel applies 59 rules from the WHO AWaRe book.

9
00:00:48,032 --> 00:00:52,030
Click any fact, and RxLint shows the pixels it was read from.

10
00:00:52,698 --> 00:00:54,075
The verdict is REVIEW.

11
00:00:54,105 --> 00:01:00,140
The critical finding shows what was read: 400 per 5 prescribed, 250 per 5 dispensed.

12
00:01:00,170 --> 00:01:04,966
The calculation is right there: 80 milligrams per millilitre against 50.

13
00:01:04,996 --> 00:01:10,624
The rule source is quoted verbatim: every finding links the pixels, the arithmetic, and the rule.

14
00:01:11,278 --> 00:01:17,155
Nemotron 3 Ultra explains the result to the caregiver, in English, French or Arabic.

15
00:01:17,185 --> 00:01:20,448
It writes around locked values, so it can never invent a number.

16
00:01:20,478 --> 00:01:26,248
And Nemotron 3 Nano audits every sentence against the verified result before it is shown.

17
00:01:26,906 --> 00:01:33,619
When the handwriting is overwritten, 2.5 or 7.5 millilitres, RxLint does not guess.

18
00:01:33,649 --> 00:01:39,489
It returns CANNOT VERIFY, and Nemotron 3 Ultra picks the single smallest question to ask.

19
00:01:39,519 --> 00:01:43,859
Reviewed values are confirmed together, with explicit choices for ambiguity.

20
00:01:43,889 --> 00:01:47,843
The rules re-run without another model call, and the case passes.

21
00:01:48,510 --> 00:01:55,430
The rule pack is frozen and hashed, so a separate live plane asks: has a regulator published anything since?

22
00:01:55,460 --> 00:02:01,430
For this French dispensing check, Tavily searches ANSM for pages naming the exact lot.

23
00:02:01,460 --> 00:02:07,832
For this bottle, dispensed in February 2019, ANSM had recalled lot JA0287.

24
00:02:07,862 --> 00:02:11,556
The live state sits next to the verdict; it never changes it.

25
00:02:12,202 --> 00:02:17,568
On 120 held-out cases, RxLint accepted zero incorrect high-risk readings.

26
00:02:17,598 --> 00:02:24,307
Forty-five percent of cases need confirmation, and label-strength accuracy is 93.3 percent.

27
00:02:24,337 --> 00:02:33,356
95 percent exact verdicts after simulated confirmation, against 85.8 when readings are trusted, with nine of ten overwritten doses held.

28
00:02:34,022 --> 00:02:38,953
A model may transcribe, report a competing reading, and write around locked values.

29
00:02:38,983 --> 00:02:42,551
It may not compute a dose, decide the verdict, or write a number.

30
00:02:42,581 --> 00:02:51,579
RxLint: Nemotron on Nebius Token Factory reads and explains, a deterministic kernel decides, and Tavily watches the regulators.
